1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,638
This isn't a road.

2
00:00:02,189 --> 00:00:03,498
It isn't a river.

3
00:00:04,048 --> 00:00:06,367
And it wasn't there before the storm.

4
00:00:07,317 --> 00:00:12,220
That pale line crossing Alabama was carved
into the landscape by a tornado.

5
00:00:13,169 --> 00:00:18,356
Five days after the disaster, a NASA
satellite photographed the scar from

6
00:00:18,356 --> 00:00:18,783
space.

7
00:00:19,733 --> 00:00:21,722
But this wasn't the only line.

8
00:00:22,272 --> 00:00:27,004
In April 2011, the American South was
crossed by many of them.

9
00:00:28,704 --> 00:00:33,584
Today, we'll follow the satellite
evidence, from clouds before the outbreak

10
00:00:33,584 --> 00:00:35,667
to the damage visible afterward.

11
00:00:36,216 --> 00:00:41,116
And we'll see how images taken hundreds of
miles above Earth reveal something a

12
00:00:41,116 --> 00:00:44,960
ground-level camera can barely show: the
scale of destruction.

13
00:00:46,661 --> 00:00:50,648
From April twenty-fifth through
twenty-eighth, twenty-eleven, the

14
00:00:50,648 --> 00:00:55,003
southeastern United States experienced a
catastrophic tornado outbreak.

15
00:00:55,954 --> 00:01:00,974
NOAA's retrospective count lists three
hundred and sixty-two confirmed tornadoes

16
00:01:00,974 --> 00:01:02,292
over those four days.

17
00:01:02,841 --> 00:01:06,510
An estimated three hundred and twenty-one
people were killed.

18
00:01:07,459 --> 00:01:11,959
Those numbers describe a human disaster,
not a spectacle.

19
00:01:12,509 --> 00:01:17,574
Behind every line on a satellite image
were neighborhoods, roads, homes, and

20
00:01:17,574 --> 00:01:18,174
families.

21
00:01:19,873 --> 00:01:22,093
Before the scars came the storms.

22
00:01:23,043 --> 00:01:27,906
NOAA's GOES satellite watched massive
thunderstorm complexes move across the

23
00:01:27,906 --> 00:01:29,186
southeastern states.

24
00:01:30,135 --> 00:01:35,400
From this distance, individual tornado
funnels are generally far too small to

25
00:01:35,400 --> 00:01:36,015
identify.

26
00:01:36,566 --> 00:01:41,564
Instead, the satellite records the
enormous cloud systems that can produce

27
00:01:41,564 --> 00:01:41,902
them.

28
00:01:42,852 --> 00:01:45,165
Look at the moving cloud towers.

29
00:01:45,715 --> 00:01:50,686
The storm field grows, shifts, and keeps
generating new intense cells.

30
00:01:51,635 --> 00:01:56,123
The dangerous conditions lasted for hours,
across a wide area.

31
00:01:56,673 --> 00:02:01,314
A single dramatic tornado video could
never reveal that bigger picture.

32
00:02:03,015 --> 00:02:05,118
Now move down to Alabama.

33
00:02:06,069 --> 00:02:11,451
Here is the region before the outbreak,
photographed by NASA's Aqua satellite on

34
00:02:11,451 --> 00:02:12,394
April twelfth.

35
00:02:13,343 --> 00:02:15,129
Green vegetation.

36
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:16,895
Urban areas.

37
00:02:17,444 --> 00:02:19,867
A familiar-looking landscape.

38
00:02:20,818 --> 00:02:25,758
Now compare it with April twenty-eighth,
the day after the April twenty-seventh

39
00:02:25,758 --> 00:02:26,383
tornadoes.

40
00:02:27,334 --> 00:02:30,346
Thin brown trails slash across the
greenery.

41
00:02:31,295 --> 00:02:32,901
These are not clouds.

42
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:38,492
They're patches of exposed and disturbed
ground, where trees and plants had been

43
00:02:38,492 --> 00:02:39,060
uprooted.

44
00:02:40,009 --> 00:02:44,515
The difference between the two images
matters: the tracks were not present

45
00:02:44,515 --> 00:02:45,550
before the storm.

46
00:02:47,250 --> 00:02:51,028
One of the clearest examples runs across
Tuscaloosa.

47
00:02:51,979 --> 00:02:57,613
On May second, NASA's Earth Observing One
satellite captured this natural-color

48
00:02:57,613 --> 00:02:57,969
view.

49
00:02:58,919 --> 00:03:03,887
The tan corridor stretches southwest to
northeast, cutting through the city.

50
00:03:04,836 --> 00:03:09,600
The National Weather Service described a
damage path of roughly eighty miles,

51
00:03:09,600 --> 00:03:12,445
reaching as much as one and a half miles
wide.

52
00:03:13,395 --> 00:03:17,521
Not every part of that full path fits
inside this one photograph.

53
00:03:18,072 --> 00:03:20,978
This frame shows a section near
Tuscaloosa.

54
00:03:21,927 --> 00:03:24,010
Zoom in on the pale corridor.

55
00:03:24,961 --> 00:03:29,949
It runs south of Bryant-Denny Stadium, and
north of University Mall.

56
00:03:30,498 --> 00:03:35,360
The location matters: this is not an
anonymous streak in empty wilderness.

57
00:03:35,911 --> 00:03:38,108
It's a line through a living city.

58
00:03:39,057 --> 00:03:41,997
And there's a trick hiding in the same
photograph.

59
00:03:42,546 --> 00:03:46,905
Running roughly parallel to the scar is a
thin airplane contrail.

60
00:03:47,455 --> 00:03:49,802
One feature is in the atmosphere.

61
00:03:50,353 --> 00:03:52,141
The other is on the ground.

62
00:03:53,092 --> 00:03:56,264
The satellite record helps us separate the
two.

63
00:03:57,965 --> 00:04:02,388
Other instruments tell the same story in
different visual languages.

64
00:04:03,337 --> 00:04:08,979
NASA's Terra satellite used ASTER to image
another section of the damage, closer to

65
00:04:08,979 --> 00:04:10,747
Birmingham, on May fourth.

66
00:04:11,697 --> 00:04:16,097
In its false-color view, healthy
vegetation appears red, and the tornado

67
00:04:16,097 --> 00:04:18,663
track cuts through as a pale beige stripe.

68
00:04:19,613 --> 00:04:23,935
Change the sensor, change the colors, but
the evidence remains.

69
00:04:24,886 --> 00:04:30,283
Years later, USGS land-change products
built from Landsat observations marked

70
00:04:30,283 --> 00:04:35,050
long streaks of altered land cover around
Tuscaloosa and Birmingham.

71
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:39,149
These maps aren't photographs of a
tornado.

72
00:04:39,699 --> 00:04:42,970
They're measurements of what changed on
Earth's surface.

73
00:04:44,670 --> 00:04:48,367
This is what makes the satellite
perspective so revealing.

74
00:04:49,317 --> 00:04:54,834
On the ground, destruction can appear as
isolated scenes: a shattered building, a

75
00:04:54,834 --> 00:04:56,809
fallen tree, a damaged block.

76
00:04:57,759 --> 00:05:01,358
From above, those scenes connect into long
corridors.

77
00:05:02,307 --> 00:05:04,673
Yet satellite imagery has limits.

78
00:05:05,223 --> 00:05:10,439
A visible scar does not tell us every wind
speed, every injury, or exactly what

79
00:05:10,439 --> 00:05:12,089
happened at each address.

80
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:18,585
For that, meteorologists combine imagery
with storm surveys, radar, and reports

81
00:05:18,585 --> 00:05:20,411
from people on the ground.

82
00:05:21,360 --> 00:05:25,579
A satellite is one witness, not the entire
investigation.

83
00:05:27,278 --> 00:05:29,961
Look again at the image from Tuscaloosa.

84
00:05:30,911 --> 00:05:36,117
That pale line is not an abstract pattern,
and it did not form naturally over

85
00:05:36,117 --> 00:05:36,793
centuries.

86
00:05:37,343 --> 00:05:42,110
It is the aftermath of a few terrifying
minutes, captured days later from orbit.

87
00:05:43,060 --> 00:05:46,983
The most powerful part of these images may
be what they leave out.

88
00:05:47,934 --> 00:05:51,475
They cannot show us the lives disrupted
along the path.

89
00:05:52,425 --> 00:05:55,165
But they can show us how far that path
went.

90
00:05:56,865 --> 00:06:01,704
From clouds spanning states to scars
crossing cities, the April twenty-eleven

91
00:06:01,704 --> 00:06:06,668
outbreak left evidence at a scale no
single camera on the ground could capture.

92
00:06:07,617 --> 00:06:11,733
And in the satellite record, those scars
remain visible.

93
00:06:12,682 --> 00:06:14,067
Orbital Atlas.

94
00:06:14,617 --> 00:06:17,394
The story is written on the planet itself.
